# `compute_pre_erosion` strip-parallel — the last unparallelised step of
# `compute_quant_field_float`
#
# WHY: benchmarks/e5_t8_phase_scaling_2026-09-10.md measured it at 2.44 ms at
# BOTH threads=1 and threads=8 — a 1.00x speedup, the only step of that
# function with no thread scaling at all, after `fuzzy_erosion` (7.06x) and
# `per_block_modulations` (6.69x) were strip-parallelised earlier the same day.
#
# RESULT (data in the .tsv):
#   threads=8  quant_field 14.40 -> 12.40 ms  (0.861x, -2.0 ms)
#              encode_inner 102.4 -> 100.5 ms (0.981x)
#              process wall 107.1 -> 103.4 ms (0.965x)
#   threads=1  unchanged on every axis (0.997x / 1.000x / 1.000x)
#
# The -2.0 ms on the phase is the number to quote; it matches the predicted
# saving (2.44 ms serial -> ~0.4 ms across 8 threads) almost exactly. The
# encode-level 0.965x is slightly larger than the phase delta alone accounts
# for and is within run noise — do not quote it as if the phase produced it.
#
# threads=1 is unchanged BY CONSTRUCTION: the parallel path is gated on
# `effective_threads() > 1`, so a single-threaded encode takes the same call it
# always did.
#
# BYTES: unchanged. 72 cells (8 images x e{3,5,7} x threads{1,4,8}) SHA256-
# identical against the pre-change binary, which is the check that matters here
# because the hash locks alone do not exercise the new path — they would pass
# whether or not the parallel branch is correct. Plus hash locks 68/68 and the
# Libjxl byte lock 5/5.
#
# HOW THE SPLIT IS ADDRESSED, AND WHY IT NEEDS ASSERTS
#   The kernel takes a tile RECT and derives its own row range from it
#   (`y_start = tile_y0.saturating_sub(4)`, `y_end = tile_y1 + 4` when
#   `tile_y1 < height`). To make it walk output rows `[b, e)` — input rows
#   `4b .. 4e` — the strip passes `tile_y0 = 4b + 4` and `tile_y1 = 4e - 4`.
#   For a one-row strip that means `tile_y1 < tile_y0`, which looks like a bug
#   and is not: the kernel never compares them.
#
#   That indirection is why the two shape checks inside the strip loop are real
#   `assert_eq!` and not `debug_assert_eq!`. MEASURED: mutating `4 * end - 4`
#   to `4 * end` makes every non-final strip compute one EXTRA output row,
#   which `copy_from_slice` then silently truncates away — correct output,
#   wasted work, and the parity test PASSES in release because debug asserts
#   are compiled out. With real asserts that mutation fails on "strip 0: row
#   count". The other mutation (`4 * begin + 4` -> `+ 8`) was caught either way.
#
# THE PARITY TEST HAD TO BE MADE NON-VACUOUS FIRST
#   `compute_pre_erosion_strip_parallel` derives its strip height from
#   `effective_threads()`. On a single-threaded test runner that is one strip,
#   and the test would have compared the kernel against itself and passed
#   without testing anything. The body is therefore split out as
#   `compute_pre_erosion_in_strips(..., rows_per_strip)` and the test forces
#   rows_per_strip in {1, 2, 3, all} over 11 sizes that straddle the strip
#   boundary, the `height / 4` truncation, and the single-row strip.
#
# SCOPE: one image, one size, one effort, one distance, one host. The saving is
# a fixed ~2 ms of serial work at 2048²; it will scale with pixel count and be
# invisible at small sizes, and it does nothing at threads=1.
#
# STILL OPEN on this cell, in measured order (unchanged from
# e5_t8_phase_scaling_2026-09-10.md except that item 3 is now done):
#   1. `cluster_histograms` — 6.8 ms, 1.00x. Now the largest serial block.
#   2. ~8.3 ms inside `quant_field` still unattributed.
#   3. `ac_tok` (4.8 ms, 1.98x) and `pass2_write` (5.6 ms, 2.93x).
